scenarios.modelAnswer:
Five independent defects, each mapping to one production symptom.

1. **The MAC is computed over re-serialized JSON.** `request.json()` destroys the signed bytes; `JSON.stringify()` may reorder keys, change whitespace and escaping, and normalize numbers, so valid deliveries fail intermittently with 400. Capture the body as text (a raw body parser, or `await request.text()`), verify, and only then parse. Parsing first also feeds unauthenticated input to the parser, which is an unnecessary attack surface.

2. **No signed timestamp, so replay is unbounded.** The provider signs a timestamp alongside the body; reject anything outside a tolerance window (a few minutes) and include the timestamp in the signed material so it cannot be altered. A perfectly valid signature on a week-old delivery is a replay.

3. **Deduplication lives in process memory.** A `Set` is per instance and dies on deploy or restart, which is why the refund is applied twice after a release and why parallel deliveries on two instances both pass the check. Persist the provider event id with a unique constraint and reserve it *before* doing work; the loser of the race acknowledges without reapplying.

4. **The dedup check happens after the work.** Two concurrent deliveries both see `has() === false`, then both call `applyEvent`. Insert the id with a status such as `accepted` first and let a unique-constraint violation mean "already owned". Make the effect itself idempotent or version-guarded as a second line of defense.

5. **Acknowledgement is tied to slow inline work, and failures produce an unbounded retry loop.** Applying inline makes the provider time out and retry; a throw returns 500 for work that may have partially happened. Accept durably (commit the event, enqueue it), return `202`, and process asynchronously with bounded retries and a dead-letter path. Also compare with `crypto.timingSafeEqual` after a length check, and tolerate out-of-order arrivals by entity version or by refetching current state instead of assuming delivery order.

Corrected shape:

```ts
export async function POST(request: Request) {
  const raw = await request.text();
  const ts = Number(request.headers.get('x-timestamp'));
  if (!Number.isFinite(ts) || Math.abs(Date.now() / 1000 - ts) > 300) {
    return new Response('stale', { status: 400 });
  }

  const mac = crypto.createHmac('sha256', secret).update(`${ts}.${raw}`).digest();
  const given = Buffer.from(request.headers.get('x-signature') ?? '', 'hex');
  if (given.length !== mac.length || !crypto.timingSafeEqual(given, mac)) {
    return new Response('bad signature', { status: 400 });
  }

  const event = EventSchema.parse(JSON.parse(raw));
  const isNew = await store.insertIfAbsent(event.id, event); // unique id
  if (!isNew) return new Response(null, { status: 200 });    // duplicate: ack only

  await queue.enqueue(event.id);
  return new Response(null, { status: 202 });
}
```

Sources: signature validation over the raw payload and replay protection (https://docs.github.com/en/webhooks/using-webhooks/validating-webhook-deliveries), delivery handling and retries (https://docs.github.com/en/webhooks/using-webhooks/handling-webhook-deliveries), signature/timestamp/ordering semantics (https://docs.stripe.com/webhooks).
